N3 VERDICT: best collapse variable = xi/R after measured-E_g subtraction (metric 0.1485)
  subtraction improvement factor m1/m3 = 1.60 (how much removing the MEASURED gauge energy tightens the collapse on xi/R)
  -> GEOMETRY DOMINANT, COULOMB CORRECTION REAL. Both readings hold and
     neither alone is the answer:
     * xi/R already organises the raw excess (metric 0.238) -- the
       excess is primarily a SURFACE/geometry quantity;
     * removing the measured E_g tightens it by 1.60x (metric 0.148),
       and the binned table shows the improvement is concentrated at
       the largest g, where the Coulomb self-energy is largest.
     So: the excess is the gradient/surface term (2/3)E_grad/(wQ), and
     the gauge sector enters ONLY as the -(2/3)E_g piece that N2
     already isolates -- not as an independent scaling variable
     (g^2Q/R alone explains essentially nothing: metric 0.916).

